[32mcontrol[0m[36m:[0m[32mtimeout[0m [36mduration[0m[36m=[0m[36mduration[0m [36mcommand[0m[36m=[0m[36mcommand[0m
Run a command, terminating it if it hasn't finished within the given duration.

If [32mcommand[0m finishes before [32mduration[0m elapses, [32mtimeout[0m returns its result normally. Otherwise, a 
termination signal is sent to it -- the same cooperative mechanism [32m
crush:terminate[0m uses, which only a command that actually checks for it (like [32msleep[0m) will actually 
stop for; most builtins don't, and will keep running in the background 
regardless -- and [32mtimeout[0m itself fails with a timeout error.

A command left running this way isn't just background noise: crush waits for 
every spawned thread to finish before the whole session exits, so a [32mcommand[0m that never 
cooperates and never finishes on its own (an infinite loop, for example) will 
keep the script -- or the whole interactive session -- from exiting cleanly, 
even though [32mtimeout[0m itself already returned its error.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mduration[0m how long to let the command run before terminating it.
 * [32mcommand[0m  the command to run.

[4mExamples[0m
[32mtimeout[0m [36m$([0m[36mduration[0m[36m:[0m[36mof[0m [36mseconds[0m[36m=[0m[36m5[0m[36m)[0m [36m{[0m[32msleep[0m [36m$([0m[36mduration[0m[36m:[0m[36mof[0m [36mseconds[0m[36m=[0m[36m30[0m[36m)[0m[36m}[0m

[32mcontrol[0m[36m:[0m[32mtimer[0m [36mit[0m[36m=[0m[36mcommand[0m
Execute a command once and return the execution time.

[4mOutput[0m
[32mduration[0m
This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mit[0m the command to time.

[4mExamples[0m
[32mtimer[0m [36m{[0m[32mfiles[0m[36m|[0m[32msort[0m [36msize[0m[36m}[0m

[32mcontrol[0m[36m:[0m[32mtry[0m [36mbody[0m[36m=[0m[36mcommand[0m [36m[[0m[36mr[0m[33m#catch=string] [catch_clause=command][0m
Execute a command, recovering from any error it produces.

If [32mbody[0m fails, execution of [32mbody[0m stops at the failing statement, and [32mcatch[0m (if given) is 
invoked instead, receiving a struct describing the error as its single unnamed 
argument: [32mmessage[0m (the error text), [32mtype[0m (the internal error variant's name, e.g. [32mIOError[0m), and [32m
command[0m (the name of the command that failed, if known -- empty otherwise). Either way, 
the error does not propagate past [32mtry[0m -- execution continues normally with whatever 
comes after it, the same as if [32mcatch[0m had been given but was empty.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mbody[0m         the command to attempt.
 * [32mcatch_clause[0m the command to invoke if [32mbody[0m fails, receiving a struct describing the error 
                (message/type/command) as its unnamed argument.

[4mExamples[0m
[32mtry[0m [36m{[0m
  [32mrisky[0m[36m:[0m[32mcommand[0m
[36m}[0m [36mcatch[0m [36m{[0m
  [36m|[0m[36m$error[0m[36m|[0m [32mecho[0m [36m([0m[36m"Recovered: {}"[0m[36m:[0m[36mformat[0m[36m([0m[36m$error[0m[36m:[0m[36mmessage[0m[36m)[0m[36m)[0m
[36m}[0m

