[32mdns[0m[36m:[0m[32mnameserver[0m
List of default nameservers

Reads the nameservers configured in [32m/etc/resolv.conf[0m -- no network query is made. This is 
exactly what [32mdns:query[0m/[32mdns:query_reverse[0m themselves fall back to when their own [32mnameserver[0m argument is left unset 
(specifically, the first one listed).


[32mdns[0m[36m:[0m[32mquery[0m [36mname[0m[36m=[0m[36mstring[0m [36m[[0m[36mrecord_type[0m[36m=[0m[36mstring[0m[36m][0m [36m[[0m--tcp[36m][0m [36m[[0m[36mnameserver[0m[36m=[0m[36mstring[0m[36m][0m [36m[[0m[36mport[0m[36m=[0m[36minteger[0m[36m][0m [36m[[0m--no_follow_cname[36m][0m [36m[[0m[36mtimeout[0m[36m=[0m[36mduration[0m[36m][0m
Look up a DNS record

The columns of the returned table depend on [32mrecord_type[0m:

 * [32mA[0m  , [32mAAAA[0m, [32mNS[0m, [32mPTR[0m, and [32mCNAME[0m: [32mtarget[0m (string) and [32mttl[0m (duration)
 * [32mMX[0m : [32mtarget[0m, [32mpreference[0m (integer), and [32mttl[0m
 * [32mSRV[0m: [32mtarget[0m, [32mpriority[0m, [32mweight[0m, [32mport[0m (all integer), and [32mttl[0m
 * [32mTXT[0m: [32mtext[0m (binary) and [32mttl[0m
 * [32mSOA[0m: [32mmname[0m, [32mrname[0m (strings), [32mserial[0m (integer), and [32mrefresh[0m, [32mretry[0m, [32mexpire[0m, and [32mttl[0m (all durations)

If a response's first answer is a [32mCNAME[0m record, it's followed transparently and the 
query is retried against the alias's target -- up to 8 hops, after which a chain 
that still hasn't resolved is a hard error rather than being followed forever (a 
spoofed or compromised nameserver could otherwise cause unbounded recursion, 
since plain UDP DNS has no cryptographic integrity). Set [32mno_follow_cname=$true[0m, or query for the [32m
CNAME[0m record type directly, to see the alias itself instead of following it.

If [32mnameserver[0m isn't given, the first nameserver listed in [32m/etc/resolv.conf[0m is used (see [32mdns:nameserver[0m). [32mname[0m is always 
looked up exactly as given -- unlike most system resolvers, this does not 
consult [32m/etc/resolv.conf[0m's search domains (see [32mdns:search_paths[0m) to expand an unqualified name.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mname[0m            DNS record to look up.
 * [32mrecord_type[0m     (default: [32mA[0m, allowed: [32mA[0m, [32mAAAA[0m, [32mCNAME[0m, [32mMX[0m, [32mNS[0m, [32mPTR[0m, [32mSOA[0m, [32mSRV[0m, [32mTXT[0m) DNS record type.
 * [32mtcp[0m             (default: [32mfalse[0m) use TCP as the transport instead of UDP.
 * [32mnameserver[0m      the nameserver to talk to. If none is given, use the nameservers configured 
                   in [32m/etc/resolv.conf[0m.
 * [32mport[0m            (default: [32m53[0m) port to talk to the nameserver on.
 * [32mno_follow_cname[0m (default: [32mfalse[0m) if a CNAME record is encountered, do not follow it. Show the 
                   actual CNAME record instead.
 * [32mtimeout[0m         (default: [32m$duration:of seconds=5[0m) connection timeout.

[4mExamples[0m
[32mdns[0m[36m:[0m[32mquery[0m [36m"www.google.com"[0m [36mAAAA[0m
[32mdns[0m[36m:[0m[32mquery[0m [36m"example.com"[0m [36mMX[0m
[33m# See the alias itself instead of transparently following it[0m
[32mdns[0m[36m:[0m[32mquery[0m [36m"www.example.com"[0m [36mCNAME[0m

[32mdns[0m[36m:[0m[32mquery_reverse[0m [36maddress[0m[36m=[0m[36mstring[0m [36m[[0m--tcp[36m][0m [36m[[0m[36mnameserver[0m[36m=[0m[36mstring[0m[36m][0m [36m[[0m[36mport[0m[36m=[0m[36minteger[0m[36m][0m [36m[[0m[36mtimeout[0m[36m=[0m[36mduration[0m[36m][0m
Perform a reverse DNS lookup on a given IP address

Looks up the hostname associated with [32maddress[0m via a [32mPTR[0m record, automatically building the 
reverse-lookup name DNS uses for this (e.g. under [32min-addr.arpa[0m for IPv4, [32mip6.arpa[0m for IPv6) -- [32m
address[0m should be given as a plain, forward IP address, not already reversed. Returns 
the hostname as a plain string, or nothing if no [32mPTR[0m record exists for that 
address. Unlike [32mdns:query[0m, this never follows a [32mCNAME[0m; a reverse zone is not expected to 
contain one.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32maddress[0m    IP address to look up. Can be either IPv4 or IPv6.
 * [32mtcp[0m        (default: [32mfalse[0m) Use TCP connection instead of UDP
 * [32mnameserver[0m Override the nameserver to talk to
 * [32mport[0m       (default: [32m53[0m) DNS port
 * [32mtimeout[0m    (default: [32m$duration:of seconds=5[0m) Connection timeout.

[4mExamples[0m
[32mdns[0m[36m:[0m[32mquery_reverse[0m [36m"127.0.0.1"[0m

[32mdns[0m[36m:[0m[32msearch_paths[0m
List of DNS search paths

Reads the DNS search domains configured in [32m/etc/resolv.conf[0m -- no network query is made. A 
system resolver normally appends these, in order, to an unqualified hostname 
before giving up (e.g. trying [32mhost.example.com[0m when asked to resolve plain [32mhost[0m); [32mdns:query[0m does not apply 
this itself -- [32mname[0m is always looked up exactly as given.


