[33m# Add an extra column to the output of files that shows the time passed since last modification[0m
[32mfiles[0m [36m|[0m [32mselect[0m [36m*[0m [36mage[0m[36m=[0m[36m{[0m[36m([0m[36mtime[0m[36m.[0m[36mnow[0m[36m([0m[36m)[0m [36m-[0m [36mmodified[0m[36m)[0m[36m}[0m

[32mstream[0m[36m:[0m[32mseq[0m [36m[[0m[36margs[0m[36m=[0m[36minteger...[0m[36m][0m [36m[[0m[36mfrom[0m[36m=[0m[36minteger[0m[36m][0m [36m[[0m[36mto[0m[36m=[0m[36minteger[0m[36m][0m [36m[[0m[36mstep[0m[36m=[0m[36minteger[0m[36m][0m
Return a stream of sequential numbers

With no arguments, seq counts forever starting at 0. Given one to three unnamed 
numbers, they're interpreted the same way Python's [32mrange[0m does: [32mseq to[0m counts from 0 up to 
(but not including) [32mto[0m; [32mseq from to[0m starts at [32mfrom[0m instead; [32mseq from to step[0m also sets the step size. The [32mfrom[0m, [32mto[0m, and [32m
step[0m named arguments below are equivalent to the two- and three-number unnamed 
forms, spelled out -- but can't be mixed with the unnamed form in the same call, 
since e.g. [32mseq 3 to=10[0m would leave it ambiguous which one actually sets the end of the 
sequence.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32margs[0m 1 to 3 numbers: [32mto[0m, [32mfrom to[0m, or [32mfrom to step[0m -- see the command's own long help. Can't be 
        combined with the from/to/step named arguments.
 * [32mfrom[0m the first number in the sequence. Defaults to 0.
 * [32mto[0m   the end of the sequence (exclusive). If not specified, the sequence will 
        continue forever.
 * [32mstep[0m the step size. Defaults to 1.

[4mExamples[0m
[32mseq[0m [36m3[0m
[33m# Prepend an index column to the output of the files command[0m
[32mzip[0m [36m$([0m[36mseq[0m[36m)[0m [36m$([0m[36mfiles[0m[36m)[0m

[32mstream[0m[36m:[0m[32mskip[0m [36m[[0m[36mrows[0m[36m=[0m[36minteger[0m[36m][0m
Skip the specified number of rows from the beginning of the stream and return 
the remainder.

[4mOutput[0m
A stream with the same columns as the input

If the stream has fewer than the specified number of rows, an empty stream will 
be returned.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mrows[0m (default: [32m1[0m) the number of rows to skip.


[32mstream[0m[36m:[0m[32msort[0m [36m[[0m[36mfield[0m[36m=[0m[36mstring...[0m[36m][0m [36m[[0m--reverse[36m][0m [36m[[0m--case_insensitive[36m][0m
Sort input stream based on one or more of it's columns

[4mOutput[0m
A stream with the same columns as the input

If any sort column contains a NaN float value, [32msort[0m fails with an error rather than 
picking an arbitrary position for it, since NaN has no defined ordering relative 
to other floats.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mfield[0m            the columns to sort on. Optional if input only has one column.
 * [32mreverse[0m          (default: [32mfalse[0m) reverse the sort order.
 * [32mcase_insensitive[0m (default: [32mfalse[0m) ignore case when sorting textual columns.

[4mExamples[0m
[33m# Show the contents of the current directory, sorted first on type and then on filename[0m
[32mfiles[0m [36m|[0m [32msort[0m [36mtype[0m [36mfile[0m

[32mstream[0m[36m:[0m[32msum[0m [36m[[0m[36mfield[0m[36m=[0m[36mstring[0m[36m][0m
Calculate the sum for the specific column across all rows.

If the input only has one column, the column name is optional.

The column contents must be exactly one of the types [32m$integer[0m, [32m$float[0m, or [32m$duration[0m.

Normally, [32msum[0m expects the type of the column to be the type to sum over, but [32msum[0m can 
also sum over a column of type [32many[0m, so long as there is at least one row in the 
table, and all the rows are actually of the same type.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mfield[0m The name of the column to find the sum of

[4mExamples[0m
[32mhost[0m[36m:[0m[32mprocs[0m [36m|[0m [32msum[0m [36mcpu[0m

[32mstream[0m[36m:[0m[32mtail[0m [36m[[0m[36mrows[0m[36m=[0m[36minteger[0m[36m][0m
Return the last row(s) of the input.

[4mOutput[0m
A stream with the same columns as the input

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mrows[0m (default: [32m10[0m) the number of rows to return.


[32mstream[0m[36m:[0m[32mtee[0m [36m[[0m[36mbranches[0m[36m=[0m[36mcommand...[0m[36m][0m
Duplicate the input stream into one or more side pipelines, passing the original 
stream through unchanged.

[4mOutput[0m
A stream with the same columns as the input

Each [32mbranches[0m block receives its own independent copy of every row and runs to 
completion as its own pipeline, in parallel with the others; its own output is 
discarded, so a branch is only useful for its side effects (writing to a file, 
counting, etc). [32mtee[0m's own output is the untouched original stream, so it composes 
directly into a larger pipeline. If a branch fails, the failure is reported via [32m
crush:warnings[0m rather than aborting [32mtee[0m or the other branches.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mbranches[0m one or more pipelines to send an independent copy of the stream through.

[4mExamples[0m
[33m# Write a snapshot to disk while continuing to filter the live stream[0m
[32mhost[0m[36m:[0m[32mprocs[0m [36m|[0m [32mtee[0m [36m{[0m[32mjson[0m[36m:[0m[32mto[0m [36msnapshot.json[0m[36m}[0m [36m|[0m [32mwhere[0m [36m{[0m[36m([0m[36m$cpu[0m [36m>[0m [36m50[0m[36m)[0m[36m}[0m

[32mstream[0m[36m:[0m[32munion[0m [36m[[0m[36mstreams[0m[36m=[0m[36many...[0m[36m][0m
Concatenate two or more streams with identical column types into one.

Every row of the first stream is output, in order, followed by every row of the 
second stream, and so on. All streams must have identical column types.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mstreams[0m the streams to concatenate.

[4mExamples[0m
[32munion[0m [36m$([0m[36mseq[0m [36m0[0m [36m3[0m[36m)[0m [36m$([0m[36mseq[0m [36m10[0m [36m13[0m[36m)[0m

[32mstream[0m[36m:[0m[32muniq[0m [36m[[0m[36mfield[0m[36m=[0m[36mstring[0m[36m][0m
Only output the first row whenever multiple rows has the same value for the 
specified column

[4mOutput[0m
A stream with the same columns as the input

If no column is given, the entire rows are compared.

This command does not just remove consecutive repeated column values, any 
repeated column values over the entire stream are removed.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mfield[0m The field to compare.

[4mExamples[0m
[32mhost[0m[36m:[0m[32mprocs[0m [36m|[0m [32muniq[0m [36muser[0m

[32mstream[0m[36m:[0m[32mwhere[0m [36mcondition[0m[36m=[0m[36mcommand[0m
Filter out rows from input based on condition

[4mOutput[0m
A stream with the same columns as the input

The columns of the row are exported to the environment using the column names, 
i.e. if the table the [32mwhere[0m command is applied to has columns [32ma[0m and [32mb[0m, then the 
environment will have variables [32mtype[0m and [32mname[0m, the variables [32m$type[0m and [32m$name[0m will be set to the 
values of the columns in the current row on each execution of the closure.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mcondition[0m the condition to filter on.

[4mExamples[0m
[33m# List all subdirectories to the current working directory[0m
[32mfiles[0m [36m|[0m [32mwhere[0m [36m{[0m[36m$type[0m [36m=[0m[36m=[0m [36mdirectory[0m[36m}[0m

[32mstream[0m[36m:[0m[32mzip[0m [36mfirst[0m[36m=[0m[36many[0m [36msecond[0m[36m=[0m[36many[0m
Combine two streams of data into one containing one row of each input stream in 
each row of output.

If the two streams have different numbers of rows, the longer stream will be 
truncated to the length of the shorter one.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mfirst[0m  the first stream.
 * [32msecond[0m the second stream.

[4mExamples[0m
[33m# Prepend an index column to the output of the files command[0m
[32mzip[0m [36m$([0m[36mseq[0m[36m)[0m [36m$([0m[36mfiles[0m[36m)[0m

[32mtypes[0m[36m:[0m[32mclass[0m [36m[[0m[36mparent[0m[36m=[0m[36mstruct[0m[36m][0m
Create an empty new class

[4mOutput[0m
[32mstruct[0m
This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mparent[0m the parent type to inherit members from (if any).

[4mExamples[0m
[33m# Create a class that represents a point in 2D space[0m
[36m$Point[0m [36m:=[0m [36m$([0m[36mclass[0m[36m)[0m
[36m$Point[0m[36m:[0m[32m__short_help__[0m [36m=[0m [36m"A point in 2D space"[0m
[36m$Point[0m[36m:[0m[32m__signature__[0m [36m=[0m [36m"class Point"[0m
[36m$Point[0m[36m:[0m[32m__long_help__[0m [36m=[0m [36m"Uses floating point numbers to represent the x and y coordinates"[0m
[36m$Point[0m[36m:[0m[32m__example__[0m [36m=[0m [36m"$p := ($Point.new(1.0, 2.0))"[0m
[33m# Constructor takes two arguments, x and y[0m
[32mPoint[0m[36m:[0m[32m__init__[0m [36m=[0m [36m{[0m
  [36m|[0m[36m$x[0m[36m:[0m[36m$float[0m [36m$y[0m[36m:[0m[36m$float[0m[36m|[0m
  [36m$this[0m[36m:[0m[32mx[0m [36m=[0m [36m$x[0m
  [36m$this[0m[36m:[0m[32my[0m [36m=[0m [36m$y[0m
[36m}[0m

[32mPoint[0m[36m:[0m[32mlen[0m [36m=[0m [36m{[0m
  [36m|[0m
  [32mshort_help[0m [36m=[0m [36m"Returns the distance from the origin"[0m
  [36m|[0m
  [36m([0m[36m$math[0m[36m.[0m[36msqrt[0m[36m([0m[36m$this[0m[36m.[0m[36mx[0m[36m*[0m[36m$this[0m[36m.[0m[36mx[0m [36m+[0m [36m$this[0m[36m.[0m[36my[0m[36m*[0m[36m$this[0m[36m.[0m[36my[0m[36m)[0m[36m)[0m
[36m}[0m
[33m# Overload the `+` operator to add two points. (Only available in expression mode)[0m
[32mPoint[0m[36m:[0m[32m__add__[0m [36m=[0m [36m{[0m
  [36m|[0m
  [32mshort_help[0m [36m=[0m [36m"Add two points together"[0m
  [36m$other[0m [36m:[0m [36m$struct[0m [36m"the other point."[0m
  [36m|[0m
  [36m([0m[36m$Point[0m[36m.[0m[36mnew[0m[36m([0m[36mx[0m[36m=[0m[36m([0m[36m$this[0m[36m.[0m[36mx[0m[36m+[0m[36m$other[0m[36m.[0m[36mx[0m[36m)[0m, [32my[0m[36m=[0m[36m([0m[36m$this[0m[36m.[0m[36my[0m[36m+[0m[36m$other[0m[36m.[0m[36my[0m[36m)[0m[36m)[0m[36m)[0m
[36m}[0m
[36m$p[0m [36m:=[0m [36m$([0m[36m$Point[0m[36m:[0m[36mnew[0m [36mx[0m[36m=[0m[36m1.0[0m [36my[0m[36m=[0m[36m2.0[0m[36m)[0m
[36m$p[0m[36m:[0m[32mlen[0m
[36m$p2[0m [36m:=[0m [36m([0m[36m$Point[0m[36m.[0m[36mnew[0m[36m([0m[36mx[0m[36m=[0m[36m-[0m[36m1.0[0m, [32my[0m[36m=[0m[36m2.0[0m[36m)[0m[36m)[0m
[36m$p3[0m [36m:=[0m [36m([0m[36m$p[0m [36m+[0m [36m$p2[0m[36m)[0m

[32mtypes[0m[36m:[0m[32mconvert[0m [36mtarget_type[0m[36m=[0m[36mtype[0m [36m[[0m[36mvalue[0m[36m=[0m[36many[0m[36m][0m
Convert the vale to the specified type

Converting a value to the type it already holds always works and returns the 
original value. Most other conversions take the input value, convert it to a 
string and then attempt to parse that string as the desired type.

If no value is provided, the value is read from the input pipeline.

The following short cut conversions exist that do not go via a string 
representation:

 * [32m$float[0m   to [32m$integer[0m the value is truncated to its integer part.
 * [32m$integer[0m to [32m$bool[0m 0 is false, all other values are true.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mtarget_type[0m the type to convert the value to.
 * [32mvalue[0m       the value to convert.

[4mExamples[0m
[32mconvert[0m [36m$integer[0m [36m1.8[0m

[32mtypes[0m[36m:[0m[32mdefinition[0m [36mcommand[0m[36m=[0m[36mcommand[0m
Returns the definition of the specified closure as a text string

[4mOutput[0m
[32mstring[0m
Returns nothing if the command is not a closure

Note that the outputted of the [32mdefinition[0m builtin is reformatted, including switching code 
between expression mode and command mode.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mcommand[0m the closure to show the definition of.

[4mExamples[0m
[33m# returns { $files --recursive '/'}[0m
[36m$all_the_files[0m [36m:=[0m [36m{[0m[32mfiles[0m --recursive [36m/[0m[36m}[0m
[32mdefinition[0m [36m$all_the_files[0m

[32mtypes[0m[36m:[0m[32mlike[0m [36mvalue[0m[36m=[0m[36many[0m [36m[[0m[36mpattern[0m[36m=[0m[36many...[0m[36m][0m
Check if the specified value matches one or more patterns.

[4mOutput[0m
[32mbool[0m
A pattern can be a string (exact match), a glob, a regular expression, a type 
(checks [32mvalue[0m's type), or any other value that implements an [32m__is__[0m method -- [32mlike[0m simply calls 
it. If multiple patterns are given, they're checked in order and [32mlike[0m returns true 
as soon as one of them matches.

In expression mode, a single pattern can also be checked via the [32m=~[0m operator (or [32m
!~[0m for the negation).

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mvalue[0m   the value to test.
 * [32mpattern[0m the pattern(s) to test the value against.

[4mExamples[0m
[33m# Match the string "fooo" against the regex ^(ooo)[0m
[32mlike[0m [36mfooo[0m [36m^(ooo)[0m
[33m# Same, in expression mode[0m
[36m([0m[36mfooo[0m [36m=~[0m [36m^(ooo)[0m[36m)[0m
[33m# A value matches if it equals any of several patterns[0m
[32mlike[0m [36mfooo[0m [36m^(ooo)[0m [36m*.txt[0m [36m"fooo"[0m

[32mtypes[0m[36m:[0m[32mmaterialize[0m
Recursively convert all streams in io to materialized form

[4mExamples[0m
[33m# Put a table of files in the current directory into the variable $f[0m
[36m$f[0m [36m:=[0m [36m$([0m[36mfiles[0m [36m|[0m [32mmaterialize[0m[36m)[0m
[33m# Because we materialized the table stream into a table, counting the elements is not a destructive operation.[0m
[36m$f[0m [36m|[0m [32mcount[0m

[32mtypes[0m[36m:[0m[32mtypeof[0m [36mvalue[0m[36m=[0m[36many[0m
Return the type of the specified value.

[4mOutput[0m
[32mtype[0m
This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mvalue[0m the value to provide the type of.

[4mExamples[0m
[33m# returns float[0m
[32mtypeof[0m [36m1.8[0m

[32mvar[0m[36m:[0m[32mget[0m [36mname[0m[36m=[0m[36mstring[0m
Returns the current value of a variable

A variable by the specified name must already exist in some visible scope before 
calling the [32mget[0m builtin, or an error will result.

The get builtin is not normally called directly, simply prefix the [32m$[0m sigil with 
the name of the variable you want to get.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mname[0m the name of the variable to return the value of.

[4mExamples[0m
[33m# These two lines are equivalent[0m
[36m$x[0m
[32mvar[0m[36m:[0m[32mget[0m [36mx[0m

var:let [<any>=any...]
Declare new variables in the current scope.

No variable can exist in the local scope, or an error will result.

The let builtin is not normally called directly, but via the syntactic sugar of 
the := operator.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mvariables[0m the variables to declare. The value you supply will be the initial value of 
             the variable.

[4mExamples[0m
[33m# These two lines are equivalent[0m
[36m$x[0m [36m:=[0m [36m2[0m
[32mvar[0m[36m:[0m[32mlet[0m [36mx[0m[36m=[0m[36m2[0m

[32mvar[0m[36m:[0m[32mlet_destructure[0m [36m[[0m[36mnames[0m[36m=[0m[36mstring...[0m[36m][0m [36mvalue[0m[36m=[0m[36many[0m
Declare new variables in the current scope by destructuring a list, struct, or 
dict.

Not normally called directly, but via the syntactic sugar of the := operator 
applied to a bracketed list of names, e.g. [32m[$a, $b] := $pair[0m. [32mvalue[0m's elements -- a list's elements, 
or a struct's/dict's values in insertion order -- are declared positionally into [32m
names[0m, in order. The number of elements must exactly match the number of names, or an 
error results.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mnames[0m the names of the variables to declare, in order.
 * [32mvalue[0m the list, struct, or dict to destructure.

[4mExamples[0m
# These two lines are equivalent
[$a, $b] := [1, 2]
var:let_destructure a b value=[1, 2]

[32mvar[0m[36m:[0m[32mlist[0m
Returns a table containing all variable names currently in scope and their 
types.

[4mOutput[0m
[32mtable_input_stream[0m [36mname[0m[36m=[0m[36m$string[0m [36mtype[0m[36m=[0m[36m$string[0m
A variable is in scope if it exists in the current scope, any of its parents, or 
any of the scopes used in any of those scopes.


[32mvar[0m[36m:[0m[32mlocal[0m
Returns the current scope.

[4mOutput[0m
[32mscope[0m

var:set [<any>=any...]
Reassign existing variables

A variable by the specified name must already exist in some visible scope before 
calling the [32mset[0m builtin, or an error will result.

The set builtin is not normally called directly, but via the syntactic sugar of 
the = operator.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mvariables[0m the variables to reassign. The value you supply will be the new value of the 
             variable.

[4mExamples[0m
[33m# These two lines are equivalent[0m
[36m$x[0m [36m=[0m [36m2[0m
[32mvar[0m[36m:[0m[32mset[0m [36mx[0m[36m=[0m[36m2[0m

[32mvar[0m[36m:[0m[32mset_destructure[0m [36m[[0m[36mnames[0m[36m=[0m[36mstring...[0m[36m][0m [36mvalue[0m[36m=[0m[36many[0m
Reassign existing variables by destructuring a list, struct, or dict.

Not normally called directly, but via the syntactic sugar of the = operator 
applied to a bracketed list of names, e.g. [32m[$a, $b] = $pair[0m. Every name must already exist in 
some visible scope, exactly like [32mset[0m. [32mvalue[0m's elements -- a list's elements, or a 
struct's/dict's values in insertion order -- are assigned positionally into [32m
names[0m, in order. The number of elements must exactly match the number of names, or an 
error results.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mnames[0m the names of the variables to reassign, in order.
 * [32mvalue[0m the list, struct, or dict to destructure.

[4mExamples[0m
# These two lines are equivalent
[$a, $b] = [1, 2]
var:set_destructure a b value=[1, 2]

[32mvar[0m[36m:[0m[32munset[0m [36m[[0m[36mname[0m[36m=[0m[36mstring...[0m[36m][0m
Removes variables from the namespace

[4mOutput[0m
[32mempty[0m
This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mname[0m the name of the variables to unset.

[4mExamples[0m
[33m# Remove the variable x[0m
[32mvar[0m[36m:[0m[32munset[0m [36mx[0m

[32mvar[0m[36m:[0m[32munuse[0m [36m[[0m[36mname[0m[36m=[0m[36mscope...[0m[36m][0m
Make specified scopes not searched by default during scope resolution.

[4mOutput[0m
[32mbool[0m
The unuse builtin will recursively go through all parent scopes and remove all 
uses of the provided scopes through the entire chain.

This command accepts the following arguments:

 * [32mname[0m the scopes to unuse.

[4mExamples[0m
[33m# Stop using the stream scope[0m
[32mvar[0m[36m:[0m[32munuse[0m [36m$stream[0m
[33m# This command will now fail, because stream::select is not in scope[0m
