[32mtype[0m [36mstring[0m
Textual data, stored as an immutable sequence of unicode code points.

A string literal is written between double quotes, e.g. [32m"hello world"[0m. A string is a sequence 
of legal unicode characters -- nothing else can be represented, and every crush 
string is guaranteed to be valid text.

Strings are immutable -- every method that looks like it modifies a string ([32m
upper[0m, [32mreplace[0m, [32mtrim[0m, ...) returns a new string rather than changing the receiver.

[32mfile[0m and [32mbinary[0m are the two other types that hold sequences of data rather than a single 
scalar value, and it's worth being precise about how they differ from [32mstring[0m and from 
each other. A [32mfile[0m represents an operating system path: a sequence of bytes that is 
a legal file name, a system-dependent notion of legality that usually allows 
byte sequences that aren't legal unicode (for example, a Latin-1-encoded name on 
a system whose text encoding is UTF-8). A [32mbinary[0m is simply a sequence of bytes with no 
legality constraint at all -- the type to reach for when the data isn't 
necessarily text or a path. Because "legal file name" and "legal unicode text" 
are overlapping but different constraints, [32mstring[0m and [32mfile[0m have to be separate types: some 
strings can't be legal file names (most commonly one containing a zero byte -- 
many operating systems represent a path internally as a zero-terminated byte 
sequence, so a zero byte can never be part of one), and some legal file names 
can't be represented as a string at all.

For convenience, builtin commands that expect a [32mfile[0m argument also accept a [32mstring[0m, which 
crush converts automatically. That conversion doesn't validate the result up 
front, though -- passing a string containing a zero byte is accepted silently, 
and only fails once the resulting file value is actually used against the 
filesystem. This isn't just an implementation shortcut: neither crush nor the 
computer it's running on can actually know what does and doesn't constitute a 
legal file name ahead of time, because that's up to whatever filesystem the path 
eventually resolves into, and that can change from one path component to the 
next -- a network mount, for instance, can enforce completely different naming 
rules than the local filesystem it's mounted under, and crush has no general way 
to know a path crosses into one before it's used. Some obviously-illegal names 
could be rejected early (a zero byte, for example, is illegal everywhere), but 
the only way to be certain a given name is legal is to actually hand it to the 
operating system in a real syscall. Going the other way, a [32mfile[0m whose bytes aren't 
valid unicode can't be losslessly converted to a [32mstring[0m either; rather than erroring, 
crush falls back to displaying it as the placeholder text [32m<invalid filename>[0m.

 * [32m__getitem__[0m     Extract a one character substring from this string.
 * [32m__is__[0m          True if the needle equals this string. Not meant to be called directly -- 
                   use the [32mlike[0m command or the [32m=~[0m operator.
 * [32m__is_not__[0m      False if the needle equals this string. Not meant to be called directly -- 
                   use the [32mlike[0m command or the [32m!~[0m operator.
 * [32mbytes[0m           Returns the length (in number of bytes) of the string, as encoded using 
                   UTF-8
 * [32mends_with[0m       True if this string ends with the specified suffix
 * [32mformat[0m          Format arguments into a string
 * [32mis_alphabetic[0m   True if every character of this string is alphabetic
 * [32mis_alphanumeric[0m True if every character of this string is alphabetic or numeric
 * [32mis_ascii[0m        True if every character of this string is part of the ascii character set
 * [32mis_control[0m      True if every character of this string is a control character
 * [32mis_digit[0m        True if every character of this string is a digit in the specified radix
 * [32mis_lowercase[0m    True if every character of this string is lower case
 * [32mis_uppercase[0m    True if every character of this string is upper case
 * [32mis_whitespace[0m   True if every character of this string is a whitespace character
 * [32mjoin[0m            Join all arguments by the specified string
 * [32mlen[0m             Returns the length (in number of unicode characters) of the string.
 * [32mlower[0m           Returns an identical string but in lower case
 * [32mlpad[0m            Returns a string truncated or left-padded to be the exact specified length
 * [32mrepeat[0m          Returns this string repeated times times
 * [32mrpad[0m            Returns a string truncated or right-padded to be the exact specified length
 * [32msplit[0m           Splits a string using the specified separator
 * [32mstarts_with[0m     True if this string starts with the specified prefix
 * [32msubstr[0m          Extract a substring from this string.
 * [32mtrim[0m            Returns a string with all whitespace trimmed from both ends
 * [32mupper[0m           Returns an identical string but in upper case


[32mtype[0m [36minteger[0m
A numeric type representing an integer number.

An integer literal is a bare number, e.g. [32m5[0m or [32m-3[0m. Underscores may be used as digit 
separators to make large numbers easier to read, e.g. [32m1_000_000[0m.

A Crush integer uses signed 128 bit precision. This means that the highest 
number that can be represented is 170141183460469231731687303715884105727, and 
the lowest is -170141183460469231731687303715884105728.

Integers are immutable values. The most similar type is [32mfloat[0m, used for numbers that 
need a fractional part; mixing an integer and a float in an arithmetic 
expression promotes the result to a float.

 * [32m__add__[0m Add this number and the specified term and return the result
 * [32m__div__[0m Divide this number by the specified factor
 * [32m__mul__[0m multiply this number and the specified factor and return the result
 * [32m__neg__[0m Negate this integer
 * [32m__sub__[0m Subtract the specified term from this number and return the result
 * [32mmax[0m     Largest integer value
 * [32mmin[0m     Smallest integer value
 * [32mmod[0m     Least positive residue after integer division
 * [32mrem[0m     Remainder after integer division


[32mtype[0m [36mtime[0m
A point in time with nanosecond precision.

To get the current time, use [32mtime:now[0m. To parse a time from text, use [32mtime:parse[0m.

All time instances use the local time zone.

A time instance has nanosecond precision. It is represented internally as two 64 
bit numbers, one for the number of seconds since the Unix epoc, and one for the 
nanosecond remainder.

Times are immutable values -- arithmetic methods like adding a [32mduration[0m return a new 
time rather than changing the receiver. The most similar type is [32mduration[0m, used to 
represent the difference between two times.

 * [32m__add__[0m Add the specified delta to this time
 * [32m__sub__[0m Remove the specified duration from this time to produce an earlier time, or 
           calculate the difference between two points in time.
 * [32mformat[0m  Format this time using a strftime-style pattern string
 * [32mnow[0m     The current point in time.
 * [32mparse[0m   Parse a time string using a strptime-style pattern string


[32mtype[0m [36mduration[0m
A difference between two points in time.

To create your own duration objects, use the [32mduration:of[0m method, for example

[32mduration[0m[36m:[0m[32mof[0m [36mseconds[0m[36m=[0m[36m10[0m
A duration instance has nanosecond precision. It is represented internally as 
two 64 bit numbers, one for the number of seconds, and one for the nanosecond 
remainder.

durations are signed, i.e. they can be used to denote a negative span of time. 
Durations are immutable values. The most similar type is [32mtime[0m: subtracting one [32mtime[0m from 
another produces a [32mduration[0m, and a [32mduration[0m can be added to or subtracted from a [32mtime[0m.

 * [32m__add__[0m          Add the specified delta or time to this duration.
 * [32m__div__[0m          Divide this duration by the specified divisor.
 * [32m__mul__[0m          Multiply this duration by the specified factor.
 * [32m__neg__[0m          Negate this duration.
 * [32m__sub__[0m          Remove the specified delta from this duration.
 * [32mdays[0m             Returns the number of days in this duration, rounded towards zero.
 * [32mhours[0m            Returns the number of hours in this duration, rounded towards zero.
 * [32mmilliseconds[0m     Returns the number of milliseconds in this duration, rounded towards zero.
 * [32mminutes[0m          Returns the number of minutes in this duration, rounded towards zero.
 * [32mnanoseconds_part[0m Returns the nanosecond part of this duration.
 * [32mof[0m               Create a new duration.
 * [32mseconds[0m          Returns the number of seconds in this duration, rounded towards zero.


[32mtype[0m [36mglob[0m
A pattern containing wildcards.

Globs are usually created by writing an unescaped string containing a wildcard 
character ([32m*[0m or [32m?[0m), like [32mfiles *.toml[0m.

If you want to construct a new glob from a string, use the [32mglob:new[0m command, e.g. [32m
glob:new "*.txt"[0m.

Globs are immutable. The most similar types are [32mre[0m, which supports much richer 
patterns at the cost of more complex syntax, and [32mstring[0m, which globs otherwise 
resemble but never match by wildcard -- only a real glob value does.

 * [32m__is__[0m     True if the needle matches this glob. Not meant to be called directly -- use 
              the [32mlike[0m command or the [32m=~[0m operator.
 * [32m__is_not__[0m False if the needle matches this glob. Not meant to be called directly -- 
              use the [32mlike[0m command or the [32m!~[0m operator.
 * [32mfiles[0m      Perform file matching of this glob.
 * [32mfilter[0m     Filter input stream based on this glob.
 * [32mnew[0m        Create a glob from a string


[32mtype[0m [36mre[0m
A regular expression is an advanced pattern that can be used for matching and 
replacing text.

Regular expressions are usually created by writing using regexp literal syntax, 
e.g. [32mfiles ^(^...$)[0m.

If you want to construct a new glob from a string, use the [32mre:new[0m command, e.g. [32m
re:new "[a-z]*\.txt"[0m.

Regular expressions are immutable. The most similar type is [32mglob[0m, which supports 
only simple wildcard patterns but with much simpler syntax.

 * [32m__is__[0m      True if the needle matches this regular expression. Not meant to be called 
               directly -- use the [32mlike[0m command or the [32m=~[0m operator.
 * [32m__is_not__[0m  True if the needle does not match this regular expression. Not meant to be 
               called directly -- use the [32mlike[0m command or the [32m!~[0m operator.
 * [32mfilter[0m      Filter input stream based on this regex.
 * [32mnew[0m         Compile a string into a new regular expression instance.
 * [32mreplace[0m     Replace the first match of the regex in text with the replacement
 * [32mreplace_all[0m Replace all matches of the regex in text with the replacement


[32mtype[0m [36mcommand[0m
A piece fo code that can be called.

The most common way to create a command is a closure literal, e.g. [32m{echo hello}[0m, or with 
named parameters, [32m{|$x| echo $x}[0m. Builtin commands are themselves command values and can be 
captured into a variable the same way, e.g. [32m$e := $echo[0m.

A command value is immutable -- calling it runs its body, but that doesn't 
change the value itself. Reassigning the variable that holds a command is a 
separate operation from mutating the command.


[32mtype[0m [36mfile[0m
Any type of file.

A file value is usually written as a bareword or single-quoted path, e.g. [32m
./Cargo.toml[0m or [32m'my file.txt'[0m -- crush recognizes these as files rather than plain strings based on their 
syntax. You can also convert an existing string explicitly, e.g. [32m
convert $file "./Cargo.toml"[0m.

A file value simply names a path; the value itself is immutable, though of 
course the file it points at on disk can change, be created, or be removed out 
from under it via methods like [32mremove[0m or commands like [32mfs:mkdir[0m. See [32mhelp string[0m for exactly how [32mfile[0m differs 
from [32mstring[0m and [32mbinary[0m, the other two types that represent a sequence of data rather than a 
single scalar value; [32mglob[0m is also related, matching a whole set of paths rather than 
naming a single one.

 * [32m__getitem__[0m Return a file or subdirectory in the specified base directory.
 * [32mchmod[0m       Change permissions of this file.
 * [32mchown[0m       Change owner of this file.
 * [32mexists[0m      True if the file exists.
 * [32mmkdir[0m       Create directory
 * [32mname[0m        The name (excluding path) of this file, as a string
 * [32mparent[0m      The parent directory of this file
 * [32mread[0m        A read source for binary_stream values
 * [32mremove[0m      Delete this file
 * [32mtouch[0m       Set the modification and access times of file.
 * [32mwrite[0m       Write a binary_stream to this file. If no stream is given, input pipe must 
               be one.


[32mtype[0m [36mtable_input_stream[0m
An input stream of table rows.

A table_input_stream is produced by any streaming command -- for example, the 
output of [32mfiles[0m or [32mseq[0m -- or by reading the [32mread[0m member of a pipe object (see [32m
(table_input_stream ...):pipe[0m, under [32mhelp pipe[0m).

It can only be traversed once: each row is consumed as it's read, so a second 
pass over the same stream sees nothing. If you need to read the same rows more 
than once, pipe the stream through [32mmaterialize[0m to turn it into a reusable [32mtable[0m. [32m
table_output_stream[0m is the writable counterpart of the same rows.

 * [32m__call__[0m    return the table_input_stream type with the specified column signature.
 * [32m__getitem__[0m Returns the specified row of the table stream as a struct.
 * [32mpipe[0m        Returns a pipe consisting of a read end and a write end.


[32mtype[0m [36mtable_output_stream[0m
An output stream of table rows.

A table_output_stream is obtained from the [32moutput[0m member of a pipe object, created by 
calling [32m:pipe[0m on a [32mtable_input_stream[0m type, e.g. [32m$p := $($(table_input_stream value=$integer):pipe)[0m; [32m$p:output[0m is then a table_output_stream that rows can be 
written to, and [32m$p:read[0m is the matching table_input_stream those same rows can be read 
back from.

Rows are written with the [32mwrite[0m method (see [32mhelp pipe:write[0m). The most similar type is [32m
table_input_stream[0m, its read-side counterpart.

 * [32m__call__[0m return the table_output_stream type with the specified column signature.
 * [32mwrite[0m    write input to this output stream


[32mtype[0m [36mtable[0m
A table of rows.

A table is created by piping a table_input_stream through [32mmaterialize[0m, e.g. [32m
files | materialize[0m.

Unlike a table_input_stream, a table is a fixed snapshot: it can be read more 
than once, indexed by row number ([32m$t[0][0m), and asked for its length ([32m$t:len[0m) -- but it has 
no methods for adding, removing, or replacing rows. The most similar type is [32m
table_input_stream[0m, the one-shot, streaming form it's materialized from.

 * [32m__call__[0m    Return the table_input_stream type with the specified column signature.
 * [32m__getitem__[0m Returns the specified row of the table as a struct.
 * [32mlen[0m         The number of rows in the table.


[32mtype[0m [36mstruct[0m
A mapping from name to value.

To create a simple immutable struct, use the [32mstruct:of[0m command, e.g. [32mstruct:of x=1 y=2[0m; its fields can be 
read ([32m$s:x[0m) but not reassigned.

To create a mutable struct that supports inheritance and methods, use the [32mclass[0m 
command; instances created from a class ([32m$MyClass:new ...[0m) do support field reassignment ([32m
$instance:x = 5[0m) from outside the class as well as from within its own methods.

The most similar type is [32mdict[0m, which is also a mapping from keys to values, but with 
keys chosen at runtime rather than fixed named fields, and no support for 
methods or inheritance.

 * [32mjoin[0m Combine any number of structs into one containing all of their members
 * [32mof[0m   Construct a struct with the specified members


[32mtype[0m [36mlist[0m [36m$string[0m
A mutable list of items, usually of the same type.

Create a list with the [32mlist:of[0m command, e.g. [32mlist:of 1 2 3[0m, or by collecting a column out of piped 
table input with [32mlist:collect[0m.

Lists are mutable: elements can be appended, removed, or replaced in place. The 
most similar type is [32mtable[0m, which is also an ordered sequence of items but where each 
item is a row of several named, differently-typed columns rather than a single 
value.

 * [32m__call__[0m    Returns a list type with the specified value type.
 * [32m__getitem__[0m Return the value at the specified index of the list.
 * [32m__setitem__[0m Assign a new value to the element at the specified index.
 * [32mclear[0m       Remove all values from this list.
 * [32mclone[0m       Create a duplicate of the list.
 * [32mcollect[0m     Create a new list by reading a column from the input.
 * [32mempty[0m       True if there are no values in the list.
 * [32minsert[0m      Insert a new element at the specified index. Following values will be 
               shifted forward.
 * [32mlen[0m         The number of values in the list.
 * [32mnew[0m         Create a new list with the specified element type.
 * [32mof[0m          Create a new list containing the supplied elements.
 * [32mpeek[0m        Return the last element from the list without removing it.
 * [32mpop[0m         Remove the last element from the list.
 * [32mpush[0m        Push elements to the end of the list.
 * [32mremove[0m      Remove the element at the specified index and return it.
 * [32mrepeat[0m      Create a list containing the same value multiple times
 * [32mslice[0m       Extract a slice from this list.
 * [32mtruncate[0m    Remove all elements past the specified index.


[32mtype[0m [36mdict[0m [36m$string[0m [36m$integer[0m
A mutable mapping from one set of values to another.

Create a dict with the [32mdict:of[0m command, e.g. [32mdict:of a=1 b=2[0m, or by collecting key/value columns out 
of piped table input with [32mdict:collect[0m.

Dicts are mutable: entries can be inserted, removed, or have their value 
replaced in place. The most similar type is [32mstruct[0m, which is also a mapping from keys 
to values, but with a fixed set of keys chosen when the struct is created rather 
than a dynamic, mutable set of keys.

 * [32m__call__[0m    Returns a dict type parametrized with the specified key and value types.
 * [32m__getitem__[0m Return the value mapped to the specified key of the dict.
 * [32m__setitem__[0m Create a new mapping or replace an existing one.
 * [32mclear[0m       Remove all mappings from this dict.
 * [32mclone[0m       Create a new dict with the same set of mappings as this one.
 * [32mcollect[0m     Create a new dict by reading the specified columns from the input.
 * [32mcontains[0m    Returns whether the given key is in the dict
 * [32mempty[0m       True if there are no mappings in the dict.
 * [32mjoin[0m        Create a new dict by merging this dict with the given dicts, keeping the 
               first value seen for any duplicate key.
 * [32mkey_type[0m    the type of the keys in this dict.
 * [32mlen[0m         The number of mappings in the dict.
 * [32mnew[0m         Create an empty new dict.
 * [32mof[0m          Create a new dict with the specified elements.
 * [32mremove[0m      Remove a mapping from the dict and return the value, or nothing if there was 
               no value in the dict
 * [32mvalue_type[0m  the type of the values in this dict.


[32mtype[0m [36mscope[0m
A scope in the Crush namespace.

A scope is normally not constructed directly -- crush creates one implicitly for 
the root namespace ([32m$global[0m) and for every closure or block invocation. The scope 
currently executing can be obtained by calling [32m__current_scope__[0m on any existing scope value, 
e.g. [32m$global:__current_scope__[0m.

Scopes are mutable: declaring a new variable ([32m:=[0m) or assignment ([32m=[0m) modifies the 
scope it's declared or resolved in.

 * [32m__all__[0m           The names of all variable visible from the current scope.
 * [32m__current_scope__[0m The current scope.
 * [32m__getitem__[0m       Return the specified member in the current scope
 * [32m__local__[0m         The names of all variables defined in the local scope.
 * [32m__name__[0m          The name of this scope, or empty if unnamed.
 * [32m__read_only__[0m     True if this scope is write protected.
 * [32m__resolve__[0m       Resolve the specified member in the current scope
 * [32m__super__[0m         The parent of this scope. The root (global) scope returns itself.
 * [32m__use__[0m           All use imports in this scope.


[32mtype[0m [36mbool[0m
True or false.

A boolean value is one of the two literals [32m$true[0m or [32m$false[0m -- there is no other way to 
construct one. Booleans are immutable, and are the result type of every 
comparison ([32m==[0m, [32m<[0m, ...) and logical ([32mand[0m, [32mor[0m) operator.


[32mtype[0m [36mfloat[0m
A numeric type representing any number with floating point precision.

A float literal is a bare number containing a decimal point, e.g. [32m5.0[0m or [32m-3.25[0m.

A Crush float is a IEEE 754 64-bit (double precision) floating point number. 
Floats are immutable values. The most similar type is [32minteger[0m; mixing the two in an 
arithmetic expression promotes the result to a float.

 * [32m__add__[0m     Add this number and the specified term and return the result
 * [32m__div__[0m     Divide this number by the specified factor
 * [32m__mul__[0m     multiply this number and the specified factor and return the result
 * [32m__neg__[0m     Negate this float
 * [32m__sub__[0m     Subtract the specified term from this number and return the result
 * [32minfinity[0m    Infinity
 * [32mis_infinite[0m True if this float is positive or negative infinity.
 * [32mis_nan[0m      True if this float is NaN
 * [32mmax[0m         Largest finite float value
 * [32mmin[0m         Smallest finite float value
 * [32mnan[0m         Not a number


[32mtype[0m [36mempty[0m
Nothing.

The instance of the empty type is returned by commands that don't return any 
value, e.g. [32mecho[0m. There is no way to construct it directly -- it only ever shows up 
as the natural result of a command that produces no output.


[32mtype[0m [36many[0m
Any type.

This is a wildcard type, matching a value of any other type. It shows up as the 
declared type of a column or argument that intentionally imposes no type 
restriction -- it is not a type user code constructs values of directly.


[32mtype[0m [36mbinary_stream[0m
A stream of binary data.

A binary_stream is obtained by reading binary data without fully loading it into 
memory first, e.g. [32mbin:from ./Cargo.toml[0m, or the [32mbody[0m of an HTTP response from [32mio:http[0m.

Like [32mtable_input_stream[0m, it can only be read once. Pipe it through [32mmaterialize[0m to get a reusable [32mbinary[0m value 
instead.


[32mtype[0m [36mbinary[0m
Binary data.

A binary value can be created by converting another value, e.g. [32m
convert $binary "hi"[0m, or by reading a [32mbinary_stream[0m to completion with [32mmaterialize[0m, e.g. [32mbin:from ./Cargo.toml | materialize[0m.

Binary data is immutable once created, and unlike [32mstring[0m or [32mfile[0m it has no legality 
constraint at all -- it's simply an arbitrary sequence of bytes, the type to 
reach for when data isn't necessarily text or a path. See [32mhelp string[0m for how the three 
sequence-of-data types ([32mstring[0m, [32mfile[0m, [32mbinary[0m) differ. The most similar type is [32mbinary_stream[0m, its one-shot 
streaming form.

 * [32m__getitem__[0m Returns the byte at the specified offset.
 * [32mlen[0m         The number of bytes in the binary.


[32mtype[0m [36mtype[0m
A type.

A type value names one of crush's own types, e.g. [32m$string[0m or [32m$integer[0m -- [32mtypeof[0m returns a value of 
this kind. Types are mostly used to declare what kind of value a signature 
parameter or table column accepts, e.g. via [32mconvert[0m or a class field declaration, 
rather than being manipulated directly by everyday scripts.

 * [32m__is__[0m     True if the needle's type is this type. Not meant to be called directly -- 
              use the [32mlike[0m command, the [32m=~[0m operator, or the [32mis[0m arm of a [32mmatch[0m block.
